-- title:
--   From HTo G G Hat Tuple Type Cons Eq
-- statement:
--   **Consing a completed outcome exposes its head bit and tail type.**
--
--   Let `params` have a field model, let `n` be a natural number, let `b` be a Boolean, let `tau` be a G-hat type of length `n`, let `g` be a completed G-hat outcome, and let `gs` be a completed outcome tuple of length `n`. The type of the tuple formed by putting `g` at the head of `gs` is the type formed by prepending `b` to `tau` exactly when the head outcome has Boolean flag `b` and the tail tuple has type `tau`:
--
--   $$
--   \operatorname{gHatTupleType}(\operatorname{cons}(g,gs))
--   =\operatorname{prependTypeBit}(b,\tau)
--   \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
--   g.\operatorname{isSome}=b\ \land\ \operatorname{gHatTupleType}(gs)=\tau.
--   $$
--
--   This identifies the fibers of the cons map by their head flag and tail type. It is the indexing equivalence used when replacing a type-filtered sum over length-`n+1` tuples by the corresponding Boolean and tail-type sums.
--
--   Formalization credit: Sirui Lu, Ruixuan Deng, Yanqiao Zhu, and Zhengfeng Ji (arXiv:2609.19814). Mathematical source: Zhengfeng Ji, Anand Natarajan, Thomas Vidick, John Wright, and Henry Yuen (arXiv:2009.12982).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The source declaration is `MIPStarRE.LDT.Pasting.fromHToG_gHatTupleType_cons_eq`; its Lean statement gives the exact quantifier order and all boundary hypotheses.
